WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:02.200
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:03.367
We're here with Ike Barinholtz,
everybody.

00:00:03.367 --> 00:00:04.534
And congratulations.

00:00:04.534 --> 00:00:05.868
"History of the World"
premiered this week.

00:00:05.868 --> 00:00:07.100
It's so funny.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.868
This is based on the classic
Mel Brooks film...

00:00:08.868 --> 00:00:11.267
-Yep.
-...who was a producer on it.

00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:12.701
So here you are with...
-Look at that.

00:00:12.701 --> 00:00:14.267
-...Nick Kroll, Wanda Sykes,

00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:17.234
our dear friend, handsome boy,
Dave Stassen.

00:00:17.234 --> 00:00:19.367
And what was it like
to take this on as a project?

00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:20.701
-I mean, look at that.

00:00:20.701 --> 00:00:22.801
We look like we're in a '50s
doo-wop crew, you know?

00:00:22.801 --> 00:00:25.234
"It's the Forks!"
[ Laughter ]

00:00:25.234 --> 00:00:27.200
It was amazing.
Mel is a legend.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.667
He's still funnier
than any of us at 96.

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:34.033
And he came to the premiere,
and he gets up there.

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:36.067
No one knew he was gonna come.
They're all surprised.

00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:38.868
And he gets up there and he
goes, "I just want you to know,

00:00:38.868 --> 00:00:42.501
on my drive over here tonight,
I listened to the radio.

00:00:42.501 --> 00:00:45.400
And as I look at you all
right now, you need to know

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.734
we're going to have continued
showers for the next two days.

00:00:48.734 --> 00:00:51.367
And there's a low-pressure front
moving in from Canada."

00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:54.467
[ Laughter ]
Amazing.

00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:58.434
-You know, we saw in the clip,
you play Ulysses.

00:00:58.434 --> 00:00:59.634
-Yes.
-Ulysses S. Grant.

00:00:59.634 --> 00:01:01.167
-Yeah.
One of our hottest presidents.

00:01:01.167 --> 00:01:03.100
-And I will say --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.300
You know, I met you almost --

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:05.901
I mean, like,
almost 30 years ago now.

00:01:05.901 --> 00:01:07.267
-Oh, my God.
-And if someone had said,

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:08.767
"He's gonna do one
of two things --

00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:10.400
he's gonna win
'Celebrity Jeopardy!'

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.000
or play Ulysses S. Grant."

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.968
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.968 --> 00:01:16.234
And the fact that you did two
in the same, like, month.

00:01:16.234 --> 00:01:17.868
-That's crazy.
-Yeah.

00:01:17.868 --> 00:01:19.634
But you actually -- He's --
You dig him.

00:01:19.634 --> 00:01:20.734
-I love him.

00:01:20.734 --> 00:01:22.868
He's the reason
we don't all say "y'all."

00:01:22.868 --> 00:01:23.834
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:01:23.834 --> 00:01:25.167
I don't want to say "y'all."

00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:26.501
I like saying "you all."

00:01:26.501 --> 00:01:27.801
"Are you all going to come?"
-Yeah.

00:01:27.801 --> 00:01:28.834
-I'm not in a hurry.

00:01:28.834 --> 00:01:30.434
-Nobody likes
hearing you say "y'all."

00:01:30.434 --> 00:01:35.567
-No! They get mad when I say it,
so I give Grant props to that.

00:01:35.567 --> 00:01:37.968
-Now, this is --
this is maybe my favorite.

00:01:37.968 --> 00:01:39.100
There's an incredible cast.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:40.300
-Amazing cast.
We got everyone.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:44.167
We got Dove Cameron,
Zazie Beetz, Jay Ellis,

00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:47.100
Taika Waititi, Jack Black.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.868
But we had one name --

00:01:48.868 --> 00:01:50.400
-I mean, I can't believe
you got him.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.534
Now, we're going to show
his head shot in a minute,

00:01:52.534 --> 00:01:54.467
because this is --
this is a cameo,

00:01:54.467 --> 00:01:56.868
and, I mean, I recognized him,

00:01:56.868 --> 00:01:59.000
but people might not,
'cause he's an up-and-comer.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.567
-He's a new ingenue
in Los Angeles.

00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:03.868
And his name is my dad.
-Yeah.

00:02:03.868 --> 00:02:06.234
-And --
-So that's Alan Barinholtz.

00:02:06.234 --> 00:02:07.334
-Alan Barinholtz.

00:02:07.334 --> 00:02:08.467
That's not what
he really looks like.

00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:09.801
He's wearing a big
prosthetic suit there,

00:02:09.801 --> 00:02:12.334
which he hated and complained
about the entire time.

00:02:12.334 --> 00:02:14.033
But he was --
You know him very well.

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:15.934
He was a lawyer his whole life.
-Yep.

00:02:15.934 --> 00:02:17.234
-And then --
-He was like the --

00:02:17.234 --> 00:02:19.400
Like, for real, whole life.
Born a lawyer.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.901
Like, he was --

00:02:20.901 --> 00:02:23.567
He looks like a man who was --
If I ever saw his baby picture,

00:02:23.567 --> 00:02:25.300
I would assume it would be like,
"At law."

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.300
-Yeah. You know, his first word
was "amicus brief."

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:33.100
But he -- he wanted to act, but
he was a lawyer his whole life.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:34.701
And then last year,
long story short,

00:02:34.701 --> 00:02:36.434
a friend of ours reached out
and was like,

00:02:36.434 --> 00:02:38.400
"Hey, we're doing a pilot.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.801
And it's a courtroom comedy,
and we thought maybe your dad

00:02:40.801 --> 00:02:42.534
could put himself on tape
to be the judge."

00:02:42.534 --> 00:02:43.868
So we put him on tape.

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:46.400
And my brother and I were like,
"He's kind of good."

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.667
And he booked it.

00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:49.734
[ Laughter ]

00:02:49.734 --> 00:02:52.267
And it wasn't a pilot,
it was a series,

00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:53.968
eight episodes for Amazon.

00:02:53.968 --> 00:02:55.667
It's called "Jury Duty."
-Yeah.

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:58.133
-And apparently,
he's amazing in it.

00:02:58.133 --> 00:02:59.601
[ Laughter ]
Everyone I know --

00:02:59.601 --> 00:03:03.300
-So, this is your dad, who lived
in Chicago, was never an actor.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.968
-Lawyer.
-Now lives in L.A.

00:03:04.968 --> 00:03:07.000
-Lives in L.A.
-Has a head shot.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:08.934
-Head shot.
Head shot.

00:03:08.934 --> 00:03:10.901
[ Cheers and applause ]
Look at that.

00:03:10.901 --> 00:03:13.767
That is an actor for hire
right there.

00:03:15.834 --> 00:03:17.300
It used to be
when he would come over,

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:18.901
he'd be like,
"Oh, how are the kids?

00:03:18.901 --> 00:03:20.801
Did you see the game?
It was amazing."

00:03:20.801 --> 00:03:23.701
Now it's like, "If you have
five minutes to put me on tape,

00:03:23.701 --> 00:03:24.834
that would be really great.

00:03:24.834 --> 00:03:27.167
I'm auditioning
for Gus Van Sant."

00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:28.934
I'm like, "Alright."

00:03:28.934 --> 00:03:30.667
-I mean, it's a wonderful story,

00:03:30.667 --> 00:03:33.133
because you and your brother are
both actors, and the fact --

00:03:33.133 --> 00:03:35.367
I mean, he must have looked at
you long enough and been like,

00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:36.734
"You know what?
They --

00:03:36.734 --> 00:03:38.367
I didn't get it from them,
they got it from me.

00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:40.100
So if they're actors, I should
get out there and do it."

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:41.267
-Well, I just like that,
you know,

00:03:41.267 --> 00:03:43.234
everyone's mad
about nepo babies now.

00:03:43.234 --> 00:03:45.334
This is a reverse nepo baby.
-Yeah.

00:03:45.334 --> 00:03:46.767
-This is a nepo parent,
which is a good thing.

00:03:46.767 --> 00:03:49.133
-You've got a nepo dad.
-I've got a nepo dad.

00:03:49.133 --> 00:03:50.934
Which everyone likes a nepo dad.

00:03:50.934 --> 00:03:54.033
It's like, "Oh, wow,
second career, good for him."

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:56.601
No one's like, "Oh, these
nepo dads are taking my work!"

00:03:56.601 --> 00:03:59.834
-I mean, again -- And I feel
like I've said a lot of things

00:03:59.834 --> 00:04:04.000
that imply negativity towards
you, but I will just say,

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.467
I'm happy your dad late in life
decided to become an actor

00:04:07.467 --> 00:04:08.934
than you decide
to become a lawyer.

00:04:08.934 --> 00:04:10.167
-It's never too late, Seth.

00:04:10.167 --> 00:04:12.100
In fact, I would like
to announce tonight

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.501
that I have opened
my own boutique law firm.

00:04:15.501 --> 00:04:17.133
If you follow me on Instagram

00:04:17.133 --> 00:04:19.167
and send me a DM
with your legal problem...

00:04:19.167 --> 00:04:21.133
-What sort of things
are you focused on most?

00:04:21.133 --> 00:04:22.868
What kind of law?
-Oh, I love it all.

00:04:22.868 --> 00:04:26.267
I love it all.
I love probate, family law.

00:04:26.267 --> 00:04:28.701
-Oh, that's great.
And you passed the bar?

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:31.634
-Bar pending.
Bar pending.

00:04:31.634 --> 00:04:32.734
But soon -- soon enough.

00:04:32.734 --> 00:04:34.367
I'm studying really hard,
I swear.

00:04:34.367 --> 00:04:36.200
-That's great, and you're going
to take the California bar.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.467
-I'm taking the California bar,
the New York --

00:04:38.467 --> 00:04:40.067
any state that will have me.
-Oh, that's lovely.

00:04:40.067 --> 00:04:42.601
-If your state has a bar,
I'm gonna take it,

00:04:42.601 --> 00:04:44.367
which is the catchphrase
for my law firm.

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:45.267
-Oh, that's right.

00:04:45.267 --> 00:04:46.634
-So if you can give me a call,

00:04:46.634 --> 00:04:48.734
I'm here to support you
in your time of need.

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:50.934
-You're coming off a little
needy when you keep saying,

00:04:50.934 --> 00:04:52.033
"If you could give me a call."

00:04:52.033 --> 00:04:53.767
-If you guys
could give me a call,

00:04:53.767 --> 00:04:55.601
I have a deal with a company

00:04:55.601 --> 00:04:57.200
that I get paid
every time you call.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.133
-AT&amp;T?

00:04:59.133 --> 00:05:02.100
-I just signed a new deal
with AT&amp;T friends and family,

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.234
unlimited plan, not Saturdays.

00:05:04.234 --> 00:05:06.067
-That's really exciting.
Thank you, buddy.

00:05:06.067 --> 00:05:07.801
It's great to see you.
You guys, Ike Barinholtz!

00:05:07.801 --> 00:05:09.467
"History of the World, Part II."

00:05:09.467 --> 00:05:11.400
Streams on Hulu, with new
episodes premiering daily

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:12.567
through March 9th.

00:05:12.567 --> 00:05:14.133
We'll be right back
with Melissa Barrera.

